5. Cryptographicly Secure Keys
Riddle me this?
Can any deterministic function create entropy?
The answer is always no, it cannot...
It is a simple reality, that no computational function can create entropy as it is deterministic or a just a variat of a PRNG source of fixed entropy. A Key Generator is nondeterministic; hence it can create entropy via external real-world entropy. Thus, a Key Generator creates CSNBRNG keys. But the entropy is still limited to the entropy of the real-world source.
Information Theoretic security requires, and is underpinned by the existence of a Cryptographic Secure Non-Deterministic Random Bit Stream generator anchored in a real-world entropy.

A Cryptographic Secure Non-Deterministic Random Bit Stream(CSNBRNG) varable elngth key is not a key or even a QRND number in the typical sense, it must be a cryptographically secure key stream QRNG, which is not derived, via any computational algorithm. The key stream has to be derived (extracted) from a physical processes (in the case for medley from a QRND source).
One cannot compute an algorithm to generate a 'key stream' as the result would be a deterministic and hence represent a predictable PRNG, not a cryptography secure key stream and be a non-deterministic key stream not computed directly from a seed/key.

